<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eugène Delacroix</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1798">1798</a>–<a href="/years/1863">1863</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ferdinand Victor Eugène Delacroix, Delacroix</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eugène Delacroix was a French Romantic artist regarded as the leader of the French Romantic school. Against the Neoclassical perfectionism of his rival Ingres, he took Rubens and the painters of the Venetian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> as models, stressing colour and movement over clarity of outline. His Barque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> was accepted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> Salon of 1822, and he went on to paint the Chios Massacre and The Death of Sardanapalus. Liberty Leading the People of 1830 was bought by the French government but removed from public view by 1832. His expressive brushstrokes and study of colour shaped the work of the Impressionists.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eugene-delacroix.jpg" alt="Eugène Delacroix" width="330" height="470" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nadar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Félix_Nadar_1820-1910_portraits_Eugène_Delacroix_restored.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ferdinand Victor Eugène Delacroix was born on 26 April 1798 at Charenton-Saint-Maurice, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. His mother, Victoire Oeben, was the daughter of the cabinetmaker Jean-François Oeben. His legal father died in 1805 and his mother in 1814, leaving him an orphan at sixteen. In 1815 he began training with Pierre-Narcisse Guérin in the neoclassical style of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacques-louis-david">Jacques-Louis David</a>. The impact of Théodore Géricault's Raft of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medusa">Medusa</a> stimulated him to paint The Barque of Dante, accepted by the Paris Salon of 1822; the public and officials largely derided it, yet the State bought it for the Luxembourg Galleries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His Chios Massacre showed dying Greek civilians rounded up for enslavement by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and expressed support for the Greek cause. The state bought it, though critics deplored its despairing tone. The Death of Sardanapalus of 1827–28, drawn from a play by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-byron">Lord Byron</a>, shows the besieged king watching as his servants, concubines and animals are killed. In 1832 he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> on a diplomatic mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> shortly after the French conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, and produced over 100 paintings and drawings of North African life.</p><p>Liberty Leading the People, painted in 1830, shows armed Parisians marching under the tricolour behind a symbolic figure of Liberty. The French government bought it, but by 1832 officials judged it too inflammatory and removed it from view; after the Revolution of 1848 Louis Napoleon put it on display. From 1833 Delacroix received many commissions to decorate public buildings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, among them the ceiling of the Galerie d'Apollon at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a> from 1848 to 1850 and frescoes at Saint-Sulpice from 1857 to 1861.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Delacroix died on 13 August 1863 after a severe throat infection, with his housekeeper Jeanne-Marie le Guillou by his side, and was buried at Père Lachaise Cemetery. His will forbade any representation of his features. At the sale of his work in 1864, 9,140 works were attributed to him, including 853 paintings and 6,629 drawings. Renoir and Manet copied his paintings, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edgar-degas">Degas</a> bought his portrait of Baron Schwiter.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eugène Delacroix” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Others prepared the way: the Romantic colourist <b>Eugène Delacroix</b>, J. M. W. Turner, the painters of the Barbizon school such as Théodore Rousseau, and Johan Barthold Jongkind, Jean-Baptiste-Camille Corot and Eugène Boudin, who painted from nature in a direct, spontaneous style and befriended the younger artists.</blockquote><a href="/movement/impressionism">Impressionism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>That year <b>Eugène Delacroix</b> painted Liberty Leading the People, after the July Revolution, and Hector Berlioz’s Symphonie fantastique was first performed.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Bartholdi and Laboulaye avoided the image of revolutionary liberty in <b>Delacroix</b>'s Liberty Leading the People, in which a half-clothed Liberty leads an armed mob.</blockquote><a href="/work/statue-of-liberty">Statue of Liberty · What It Is</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Saint-Maurice, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.82,2.44&amp;year=1840" aria-label="Saint-Maurice on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4606 459 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Eugène Delacroix"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5056 729h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5056 729h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.82,2.44&amp;year=1840">What was here in 1840? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
